Ways the change could fail a criterion (what each rating is measured against):

  context-section-optional-background
    [not_wired] context-section-optional-background/context-not-counted-as-read
      The new `context` list is added to ParsedTaskFile, but other code that checks total block coverage (for example `unread_source`) may not include it. A task file with a `## Context` section would then report unread source and be flagged or rejected.
      src/acceptance/requirement/task_file.py#2
      src/acceptance/requirement/task_file.py#3
      src/acceptance/requirement/task_file.py#4
      pairs: 60 put to the model, 56 skipped once the defect was covered
    [scope_too_narrow] context-section-optional-background/context-not-passed-on-all-decompose-paths
      Context is passed to `_ask_about` only at two call sites in `decompose`. Any other path that builds the decomposer prompt, such as the revision or retry path or other callers of `_user_prompt`, may omit it. Prompts would then differ by path.
      src/acceptance/requirement/obligations.py#5
      src/acceptance/requirement/obligations.py#6
      src/acceptance/requirement/obligations.py#7
      src/acceptance/requirement/obligations.py#1
      pairs: 1 put to the model, 0 skipped once the defect was covered
    [other] context-section-optional-background/context-only-prompt-guard-no-structural-exclusion
      Context is shown to the model, and the only barrier against deriving an obligation from it is prompt wording. Nothing structural rejects an obligation whose source quote comes from the Context text, so the model can still emit a keep-today's-behaviour obligation from background.
      src/acceptance/requirement/obligations.py#3
      src/acceptance/requirement/obligations.py#5
      pairs: 116 put to the model, 0 skipped once the defect was covered
    [missing_case] context-section-optional-background/context-heading-level-scoping-swallows-or-leaks
      Context scoping depends on heading levels. A `# Context` at level 1 would swallow later `## Constraints` and `## Scope exclusions` as background. A heading with different text, such as `Context:` or `Background`, is not recognised as Context. In both cases mandate text is misclassified.
      src/acceptance/requirement/task_file.py#4
      pairs: 4 put to the model, 4 skipped once the defect was covered

  parse-records-context-field
    [missing_case] parse-records-context-field/subheading-text-dropped-from-context
      Sub-headings nested under `## Context` are skipped with `continue` and never appended to `context`. The recorded Context content loses the sub-heading text, so background organised under `### ...` headings is incomplete.
      src/acceptance/requirement/task_file.py#4
      pairs: 4 put to the model, 112 skipped once the defect was covered
    [missing_case] parse-records-context-field/context-heading-exact-match-only
      The Context section is recognised only when the lowercased, stripped heading text equals exactly `context`. Variants such as `Context:` or `Context and background` are not recognised, so their content falls into unclaimed or the previous section instead of `context`.
      src/acceptance/requirement/task_file.py#1
      src/acceptance/requirement/task_file.py#4
      pairs: 4 put to the model, 112 skipped once the defect was covered
    [wrong_output_shape] parse-records-context-field/context-block-kinds-mis-spanned
      Context content is split into spans by block kind (paragraph, list items, other blocks via `_block_span`). Lists are broken into per-item spans and other blocks are spanned differently, so `context` may not hold the section's content as contiguous blocks in source order.
      src/acceptance/requirement/task_file.py#4
      pairs: 4 put to the model, 112 skipped once the defect was covered

  context-blocks-carry-source-spans
    [wrong_output_shape] context-blocks-carry-source-spans/nonparagraph-context-block-span-wrong
      Context blocks that are not paragraphs or lists (tables, fenced code, blockquotes) are spanned by `_block_span`. That helper may compute offsets differently from `_span`, so these spans could point at the wrong region of the source or fail to map back to the block's text.
      src/acceptance/requirement/task_file.py#4
      pairs: 4 put to the model, 112 skipped once the defect was covered
    [scope_too_narrow] context-blocks-carry-source-spans/context-list-spans-not-per-item-source
      List blocks in Context go through `_emit_list`, which appends spans to `result.context`. Nested or multi-paragraph list items may be emitted with spans that do not map back to the source, or may be dropped, so some list content has no span.
      src/acceptance/requirement/task_file.py#4
      pairs: 4 put to the model, 112 skipped once the defect was covered
    [missing_case] context-blocks-carry-source-spans/context-subheading-boundary-misrouted
      The Context section boundary depends on the heading-level tracking. Sub-headings deeper than the Context heading are skipped with `continue`. A same-level or shallower heading ends the section. If this tracking misclassifies a heading, blocks under Context are routed to other lists (`unclaimed` or `behavior`) and are not recorded in `context`, or blocks from other sections land in `context`.
      src/acceptance/requirement/task_file.py#4
      src/acceptance/requirement/task_file.py#1
      pairs: 4 put to the model, 4 skipped once the defect was covered

  context-not-reported-unread
    [not_wired] context-not-reported-unread/unread-report-ignores-context-list
      The unread-source report is computed outside this diff, from the lists it already knows (behavior, constraints, scope_exclusions, completion_expectations, unclaimed). The new `context` list is not among them, so Context blocks are never counted as read and show up as unread source.
      src/acceptance/requirement/task_file.py#2
      src/acceptance/requirement/task_file.py#3
      src/acceptance/requirement/task_file.py#4
      pairs: 12 put to the model, 104 skipped once the defect was covered
    [scope_too_narrow] context-not-reported-unread/context-container-block-span-not-leaf
      Inside Context, non-paragraph, non-list blocks (blockquotes, tables, fences) are spanned whole with `_block_span`. The module treats container nodes by descending into them, and total-coverage accounting is per leaf block. A container span may not match the leaf blocks, or may not be handled at all, so some Context content could still be reported as unread.
      src/acceptance/requirement/task_file.py#4
      pairs: 4 put to the model, 112 skipped once the defect was covered
    [missing_case] context-not-reported-unread/context-subheading-boundary-leak
      The sub-heading check `level > context_level` only holds the Context state while headings are deeper than the Context heading. A heading at the same or a shallower level ends the Context section, and a following block goes to another section or to `unclaimed`. A Context section that is not a top-level section, or one written differently, could leave some of its blocks outside `context`.
      src/acceptance/requirement/task_file.py#4
      pairs: 2 put to the model, 0 skipped once the defect was covered

  context-blocks-have-no-requirement-id-or-disposition
    [scope_too_narrow] context-blocks-have-no-requirement-id-or-disposition/deeper-mandate-heading-swallowed-into-context
      While inside a Context section, any heading deeper than the Context heading is skipped without resetting `section`. A mandate heading such as `### Constraints` or `### Task` placed after Context at a deeper level is therefore absorbed into `context`. Those blocks are then dropped from the requirement list and get no requirement id or disposition, although they are mandate.
      src/acceptance/requirement/task_file.py#4
      pairs: 4 put to the model, 112 skipped once the defect was covered
    [not_wired] context-blocks-have-no-requirement-id-or-disposition/registry-may-treat-context-as-requirement
      The diff adds `context` to `ParsedTaskFile` but does not touch `build_registry` or the code that assigns dispositions. If either iterates over all span lists, or over fields generically, Context blocks would receive requirement ids and dispositions. Nothing in the change guards against this.
      src/acceptance/requirement/task_file.py#2
      src/acceptance/requirement/task_file.py#3
      pairs: 4 put to the model, 112 skipped once the defect was covered
    [other] context-blocks-have-no-requirement-id-or-disposition/context-shown-but-obligation-derivation-not-blocked
      Context text is injected into the decomposer prompt. Only the prompt wording says no obligation is derived from it. Quotes and spans stay restricted to the requirement text, but nothing in code stops the model from deriving an obligation or disposition grounded in Context content, for example one that preserves today's behaviour.
